Z||000000||Haze_Performance||05-04-2000||12:08 AM||||I noticed today that there was a very small amount of antifreeze coming from the bottom of the thermostat housing and sitting on top of the Trans. So I went and bought a new thermostat. The question is there a gasket that gos in between the engine and the termostat housing? The parts store said NO..and the parts book shows no part # for a gasket.I also bought a make you own gasket kit. Do I have to just make one???  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||05-04-2000||03:26 AM||||?????||||reg||5
Z||000002||William Federle||05-04-2000||06:49 AM||||I have a 4 cyl 1984 Fiero and I've bought two thermostats (at different times). In both cases the store clerk tried to sell me a gasket with it - I think it's in his computer that way (autozone). I think there is a gasket there. If not then it was probably sealed with RTV like almost everything else on the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-04-2000||02:54 PM||||Fiero thermostats drop in the housing and are held down by the cap. They don't go in between the neck and the block. At least not on any stock fiero.<P>If the housing is leaking take it off and make sure its flange is close to flat. If its bent then you may need to replace it. Depends how bad it's bent.<P>If it's pretty flat then clean everything to bare mettal and use RTV for sealer. Use only enough RTV to seal it. Excess that gets squeezed to the inside can break off and plug small passages or hang up the thermostat.||||reg||1
